WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.068
-Our first guest to night is
an Emmy-nominated comedian

00:00:03.068 --> 00:00:05.138
You know her from her work on
"Saturday Night Live,"

00:00:05.138 --> 00:00:07.273
"Coming to America,"
and "Our Flag Means Death."

00:00:07.273 --> 00:00:11.143
She's hosting "The Daily Show"
January 17th through the 19th

00:00:11.143 --> 00:00:13.479
on Comedy Central,
and her podcast,

00:00:13.479 --> 00:00:17.016
"The Fckry with Leslie Jones
&amp; Lenny Marcus"

00:00:17.016 --> 00:00:18.484
airs new episodes on Thursdays,

00:00:18.484 --> 00:00:19.785
Please welcome back
to the show our good friend,

00:00:19.785 --> 00:00:22.121
Leslie Jones, everybody!

00:00:22.121 --> 00:00:24.123
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:24.123 --> 00:00:32.665 align:start size:34% position:66% line:0%
♪♪

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:41.174 align:start size:34% position:66% line:0%
♪♪

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:43.709
Leslie, as I live and breathe!
-So...

00:00:43.709 --> 00:00:45.778
Yes, let me sit
so I don't show my cooch.

00:00:45.778 --> 00:00:47.680
-Okay, thank you.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:49.783
You're very, very polite.
-Yes.

00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:53.253
-You've gone to a manners and
etiquette class, I can tell.

00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:54.787
How are you?
-I learned how to say "Cooch."

00:00:54.787 --> 00:00:57.022
[ Laughter ]

00:00:57.022 --> 00:00:59.392
-It's so nice to have you back.
-Well, thank you.

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:00.893
It's always good
to be here with you.

00:01:00.893 --> 00:01:02.595
-This is a very exciting week
for you being in New York City.

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:03.729
I want to ask about
"The Daily Show,"

00:01:03.729 --> 00:01:05.832
but I also --
Fred isn't here this week,

00:01:05.832 --> 00:01:07.600
-Fred, my husband.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.936
-Yeah, because you were on
"Our Flag Means Death,"

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:13.172
And you were Spanish Jackie, and
Fred was one of your husbands.

00:01:13.172 --> 00:01:15.541
-Yes. He was one of
my favorite husbands, too.

00:01:15.541 --> 00:01:16.742
-That's so lovely.

00:01:16.742 --> 00:01:18.077
-Because he would help me
get dressed, you know?

00:01:18.077 --> 00:01:21.113
-We would talk about
business things, you know?

00:01:21.113 --> 00:01:23.916
Sometimes he got a little touchy
in public, but it was cool.

00:01:23.916 --> 00:01:25.818
-That's cool. It's good
to have multiple husbands

00:01:25.818 --> 00:01:26.886
in case one of them runs afowl.

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:29.489
-It's good to have
multiple husbands.

00:01:29.489 --> 00:01:32.391
-Now, you got to shoot
the show in New Zealand.

00:01:32.391 --> 00:01:34.827
-Yes.
-How did you enjoy New Zealand?

00:01:34.827 --> 00:01:37.096
-I enjoyed it for the first
week I was there

00:01:37.096 --> 00:01:38.230
'cause, you know,
you're a tourist.

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:40.032
You're like, I'm in New Zealand!
-Yeah.

00:01:40.032 --> 00:01:42.668
-You insult a couple of new
Zealanders by saying,

00:01:42.668 --> 00:01:44.203
"Oh, this is just like
Australia."

00:01:44.203 --> 00:01:46.572
And then they go --
-Yeah.

00:01:46.572 --> 00:01:49.576
-And then everything is off
the land.

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:52.077
They're really fresh out there,
but they don't have, like,

00:01:52.077 --> 00:01:53.146
the same type of food.

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:55.514
It's just, like, a lot of lamb.
-uh-huh.

00:01:55.514 --> 00:01:57.450
-A lot of heavy pies.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:58.451
-uh-huh.

00:01:58.451 --> 00:01:59.985
You didn't care for the menu?

00:01:59.985 --> 00:02:02.488
-Not after a week.
After a week, I was like,

00:02:02.488 --> 00:02:04.890
I don't want another pie.
-uh-huh.

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:06.593
-I don't want another pie.
-You hit your pie limit?

00:02:06.593 --> 00:02:08.027
-I hit my pie --

00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:10.196
and I never thought
I would say those words.

00:02:10.196 --> 00:02:12.098
-Uh-huh.
-Pie limit.

00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:13.600
-Yeah.
Were you near the water?

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:14.833
-Yes.
-Were you, like, on the coast?

00:02:14.833 --> 00:02:16.335
You must have been for
"Our Flag Means Death."

00:02:16.335 --> 00:02:18.604
-And the weather was crazy
'cause

00:02:18.604 --> 00:02:20.106
you know how they sell it.

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:21.541
It was like, oh, we're doing it
in New Zealand,

00:02:21.541 --> 00:02:23.309
and it's going to be
during the summertime?

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:24.877
-Right.
-It's going to be hot out there.

00:02:24.877 --> 00:02:27.680
They said it was gonna be
beautiful, they said.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:30.450
And we got out there, and it
rained every day.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.151
And not just regular rain,
like,

00:02:32.151 --> 00:02:35.054
that rain where I called
the front desk and was like, yo,

00:02:35.054 --> 00:02:37.423
Are we safe?

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:40.526
Like seriously, how many floors
up do I need to be

00:02:40.526 --> 00:02:42.729
to not get hit by the tsunami?

00:02:42.729 --> 00:02:45.197
[ Laughter ]

00:02:45.197 --> 00:02:47.466
-And this is really cool.

00:02:47.466 --> 00:02:49.569
I'm very impressed that you
spent your time there.

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:51.104
You also did some stand-up in
New Zealand.

00:02:51.104 --> 00:02:52.471
-Yes!
-How did you find the crowds?

00:02:52.471 --> 00:02:55.608
-Oh, my goodness, so calm.

00:02:55.608 --> 00:02:58.010
[ Laughter ]

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:00.813
They were so reserved and
kicked-back,

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:03.683
but I guess you can be reserved
and kick-back in New Zealand.

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:05.551
You don't got to be crazy
Americans.

00:03:05.551 --> 00:03:09.222
They're like drinking wine and
tea and, you know --

00:03:09.222 --> 00:03:11.190
-They got big bellies
full of lamb pie.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:14.960
-You know what I'm saying?
[ Inaudible ] crazy American.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:16.763
-Because you're very
calm on stage?

00:03:16.763 --> 00:03:18.698
-No, at all.

00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:21.634
So, it was a lot of --
it was a lot of, "Whoa."

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:23.736
[ Laughter ]

00:03:23.736 --> 00:03:26.005
-Do you have a New
Zealand accent now?

00:03:26.005 --> 00:03:28.240
-Oh, that's so cruel
for you to do that.

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:31.510
The only thing I know how to do
is say "no" in New Zealand.

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:33.212
-Okay.
-Naw-o.

00:03:33.212 --> 00:03:34.714
[ Laughter ]

00:03:34.714 --> 00:03:37.216
-So it's four syllables.
-Yes.

00:03:37.216 --> 00:03:39.752
Nah-o.

00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:41.654
-So you've had a podcast
with your friend Lenny.

00:03:41.654 --> 00:03:42.855
I have been lucky enough
to do it.

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:44.757
It's a blast. Tell me how --

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:46.258
You guys obviously have
a great chemistry.

00:03:46.258 --> 00:03:48.661
How long have you known Lenny,
and how did you meet him?

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:50.196
-Well, you know at The Cellar,

00:03:50.196 --> 00:03:52.565
you know, when you sit
at Estee's table,

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:54.500
you meet, like, a little,
you know --

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:55.868
-Comedy cellar here in
New York.

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:58.738
-Yeah, a caravan of comedians
that's always sitting

00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:01.040
at the table, and Lenny
was there one year

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.009
I think when I first got "SNL."

00:04:03.009 --> 00:04:05.378
He was there, and he was talking
about this girl he was dating.

00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:07.780
And I was like, "Oh, wow, this
girl, you should definitely,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.081
you know, be with this girl.

00:04:09.081 --> 00:04:11.717
Then the next year I came in
because you know "SNL"

00:04:11.717 --> 00:04:12.952
keeps you busy.
-Yeah.

00:04:12.952 --> 00:04:14.553
-I saw him the next --
the following year,

00:04:14.553 --> 00:04:16.055
and he's like
getting married to her.

00:04:16.055 --> 00:04:18.724
And I was like, yeah, you know,
get -- marry the girl, whatever.

00:04:18.724 --> 00:04:21.960
Then I think the third year,
between that and --

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:23.930
they were sitting there
watching the Yankees,

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:25.932
and they was watching
it on TV, cheering.

00:04:25.932 --> 00:04:28.467
I was like, I can get seats to
the Yankees.

00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:30.269
So I took them to
Lauren's seats.

00:04:30.269 --> 00:04:31.471
-Wow, those are good seats.

00:04:31.471 --> 00:04:33.072
Those are the good seats.
-Oh, man, we had --

00:04:33.072 --> 00:04:35.708
We lau-- I just, like, laughed.

00:04:35.708 --> 00:04:39.645
We were just laughing, like just
easy laugh and kicking it.

00:04:39.645 --> 00:04:42.314
He had already gave me some
notes like, you know,

00:04:42.314 --> 00:04:44.984
when we came off the stage
he had, like, this little,

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:46.285
you know nerds.
-Yeah.

00:04:46.285 --> 00:04:49.221
-He had the whole -- you know,
he had the whole like --

00:04:49.221 --> 00:04:50.389
-Yeah, they write small.
-Really small!

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:51.524
Nerds like that
tiny handwriting.

00:04:51.524 --> 00:04:52.959
-Really small.
Really small.

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:55.194
Like my whole set was on this
little bitty piece of paper.

00:04:55.194 --> 00:04:56.595
Then he had equations by it.

00:04:56.595 --> 00:04:58.030
-It's no fair because
they have glasses.

00:04:58.030 --> 00:04:59.699
So their glasses can see
tiny writing, yeah.

00:04:59.699 --> 00:05:01.834
-Especially his.
His is especially nerdy.

00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:04.770
You know what I'm saying?
So he has like a nerd --

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:09.142
But he gave me like some notes,
and I used 8 out of 10

00:05:09.142 --> 00:05:10.943
of the jokes.
And I was like, "Oh, dude."

00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:13.178
So we were sitting at the game,
and I was like, yo, dude,

00:05:13.178 --> 00:05:14.680
you want to come
and open for me?

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.882
And he came and opened up for
me, and that was it.

00:05:16.882 --> 00:05:19.252
-That's great.
And now you have a budding
friendship, which is --

00:05:19.252 --> 00:05:20.753
-Yes.
-Which is fantastic.

00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:23.288
-And we write so well
together.

00:05:23.288 --> 00:05:24.357
-That's really great.

00:05:24.357 --> 00:05:25.558
It's lucky to have
someone like that.

00:05:25.558 --> 00:05:27.493
-When he was there, we
were talking about --

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:30.530
you're very kind, talking about
how I'm the greatest living

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:34.099
American impressionist.
-Which you are, though.

00:05:34.099 --> 00:05:35.534
-Thank you very much.
I don't brag about it.

00:05:35.534 --> 00:05:36.568
That's not my style.

00:05:36.568 --> 00:05:38.104
-The Obama one
is my favorite one.

00:05:38.104 --> 00:05:39.405
I'm sorry.
-But you --

00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:42.141
You were talking about --
you know I do an Al Pacino,

00:05:42.141 --> 00:05:44.143
but you have an Al Pacino story
that I would very much

00:05:44.143 --> 00:05:45.945
like you to share
when you saw it.

00:05:45.945 --> 00:05:47.947
-He's gonna take me tell it.
-It's just too good.

00:05:47.947 --> 00:05:51.717
-Okay. So -- okay.

00:05:51.717 --> 00:05:57.090
So first of all, y'all need to
know I'm ignorant, okay?

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:00.827
I have no chill whatsoever.

00:06:00.827 --> 00:06:03.062
So we're standing in front of
this hotel,

00:06:03.062 --> 00:06:05.631
And I -- you know, I
spot Al Pacino.

00:06:05.631 --> 00:06:08.334
And I go over, and I'm like,
"Oh, my God. I love you."

00:06:08.334 --> 00:06:10.402
I think you're one of the best
actors in this business.

00:06:10.402 --> 00:06:12.671
You know, I'm bowing to him, and
I was like,

00:06:12.671 --> 00:06:14.607
the work that you did in
"The Godfather" and woo, woo.

00:06:14.607 --> 00:06:17.209
You're just so woo, woo, woop,
woop, and I'm going off.

00:06:17.209 --> 00:06:21.080
And then he looks up at me.
He goes --

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:23.816
he just looks, and then he
walks off and gets into the car.

00:06:23.816 --> 00:06:26.119
And then my friend comes over,
and he goes,

00:06:26.119 --> 00:06:28.020
you know that was
Dustin Hoffman, right?

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:30.056
[ Laughter ]

00:06:33.292 --> 00:06:35.594
And Dustin Hoffman, he was
like --

00:06:35.594 --> 00:06:37.262
Dustin Hoffman was probably
like, damn,

00:06:37.262 --> 00:06:38.564
Latifa be trippin'.

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:41.300
[ Laughter ]

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:42.801
-I got a lot more to ask
you about.

00:06:42.801 --> 00:06:44.670
This week's host of
"The Daily Show."

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:46.572
We'll be right back
with Leslie Jones.

